Date: Mon, 15 Jun 2026 09:40:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC v3 0/4] net: flow_dissector: byte-identical fast-path for eth + IP + TCP/UDP (~40-50%, x86/ARM/RISC-V)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The short version
=================

The flow dissector runs on close to every packet Linux forwards or
shapes: skb_get_hash() from RPS/RFS, the fq / fq_codel / cake qdiscs,
tc-flower, RSS software fallback, and more. The overwhelmingly common
packet shape it walks is eth + IP + TCP/UDP - and today it walks it
with a generic, protocol-graph parser (loop iterations, per-field
__skb_header_pointer, indirect dispatch).

This series adds a straight-line fast-path for exactly that common
shape - IPv4 and IPv6, TCP and UDP - that is **byte-identical** with
the slow path: it either produces bit-for-bit the same flow keys, or
it defers to the slow path. There is no third outcome. Anything it
does not handle (IPv4 options/fragments, IPv6 extension headers,
requested non-zero flow label, any non-eth/IP/L4 shape) falls through
to the existing code unchanged.

Measured **~40-50% reduction in dissector cost** across AMD/Intel x86,
three ARM uarches, and RISC-V - largest on the narrow in-order cores
RISC-V is now bringing into networking. Because it is byte-identical,
it is safe to enable broadly; we think there is a real argument for
enabling the common-case shape by default. It ships opt-in
(default-off; one not-taken branch when off) behind per-shape sysctls
under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/ so it can be adopted conservatively
- net.flow_dissector.eth_ip is the common-case knob, and extension
shapes (vlan, qinq, ...) stack on it.

(Supersedes v1, applied to net-next as eeca3eb493b8, and v2-experiment,
8013aee91ccb. Both withdrawn; this replaces them.)

Cross-architecture measurements
===============================

Userspace libflowdis A/B - identical dissector source built two ways
(fast-path .so vs baseline .so), so it isolates the dissector from the
NIC / qdisc / scheduler. Synthetic eth+IPv4+TCP, 10 x 10 M iterations,
core-pinned, ns/pkt (lower is better):

    uarch                          baseline  patched  speedup
    x86 Zen 2  (TR PRO 3945WX)       12.44     6.56    -47.3%
    x86 Skylake (i9-10885H)          10.61     5.62    -47.0%
    ARM Cortex-A53 (pi3-1)          118.97    61.27    -48.5%
    ARM Cortex-A72 (pi4-1)           42.37    19.05    -55.0%
    ARM Cortex-A76 (pi5-2)           19.37     9.25    -52.3%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60 (bpi-f3)     98.16    49.27    -49.8%

IPv6 - the other ~half of real traffic - is covered by the same knob
and is also byte-identical (verified: same addrs, addr_type, ports):
x86 Zen2 eth+IPv6+TCP -39% (vs -42% IPv4); RISC-V X60 -25% (vs -50%).
The smaller IPv6 win on the narrow core is the unavoidable 32-byte
IPv6 address copy - both paths must do it - dominating the v6
fast-path; x86's wider core hides it, so v6 ~ v4 there.

Two honest notes from the data:

  - The win is algorithmic - the fast-path retires roughly half the
    instructions (RISC-V X60: 220 -> 112 insn/pkt; x86 Zen2: 175 ->
    99) - so it reproduces across ISAs. Per-shape detail and the full
    cross-arch matrix (eth_ip/vlan/qinq/pppoe/mpls/ipip x 7 uarches)
    are in the xdp2 repo perf-results/2026-06-25-series3-allshapes-
    microbench/ and .../riscv-deep-dive/.

  - One exception: x86 Zen 1 (Ryzen 2400G) shows ~0% at p50 - the
    saving is masked on that older uarch.

Why narrow cores gain most (RISC-V)
-----------------------------------

The X60 runs at IPC ~1.4, flat between slow and fast: a narrow core
cannot hide the slow path's extra instructions behind ILP, so each
removed instruction removes a cycle ~1:1 (-50%). Zen2 at IPC ~4.2
actually runs the *slow* path at slightly higher IPC than the fast
path - its wide OoO engine absorbs some of the slow path's redundancy
as ILP, masking it - so its relative win is smaller (-41%). In
absolute terms the X60 spends ~8x the wall-clock per dissect (~98 ns
vs ~10 ns), so the fast-path returns ~48 ns/pkt to RISC-V vs ~4 ns to
x86. As the kernel's hot paths increasingly run on narrower edge cores
than the x86 servers they were tuned on, leaning them out is
disproportionately valuable - and measurable today.

The case for enabling the common case by default
================================================

  - Safe: byte-identical, by construction (same keys, or defer). It
    cannot change observable behaviour, only speed.
  - Broad: eth + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP is the shape of nearly all Linux
    traffic, and the dissector runs on it per-packet on any host doing
    software hashing/classification (RPS, fq/cake, tc-flower).
  - Cheap when off: one not-taken branch per call.

The series still ships default-off/opt-in by netdev convention; the
data above is offered as the argument to reconsider that default for
the common-case (eth_ip) shape.

Real-world relevance - WireGuard
================================

Not just a synthetic shape. WireGuard's encrypted transport is
eth + IP + UDP (the eth_ip shape), and real WireGuard servers/gateways
run it through cake/fq for QoS, which classifies every packet via
skb_get_hash() -> the flow dissector. Measured on a live WireGuard
sender (Cortex-A76, cake on egress, 902 Mbit/s over wg0):
__skb_flow_dissect fired 1,111,528 times in 10 s (~once per wire
packet) - dominated by cake_enqueue on the physical TX path, plus
tc_classify and RPS. WireGuard also force-calls skb_get_hash() on the
inner packet before encryption (drivers/net/wireguard/send.c). So the
fast-path applies to ~100% of WireGuard traffic on any host doing
software qdisc/RPS, returning per-packet CPU to the crypto that is the
actual bottleneck. (Detail: perf-results/2026-06-25-wireguard-flowdis/.)

Honest caveats
==============

  - The macro (live-traffic) A/B is link- or queue-bound on our
    testbeds, so the dissector saving shows up as receiver CPU
    headroom, not throughput, and sits within run-to-run noise at
    single-cell granularity. (On the RISC-V board the in-tree
    single-RX-queue k1_emac queue-saturates at ~17% CPU, so a flood
    cannot even make the dissector the bottleneck - the microbench is
    the right instrument.) The per-packet win is real; it converts to
    throughput only where the CPU, not the link/queue, is the limit.
  - Absolute ns/pkt above are per-ISA baselines; the cross-arch
    *ratios* are the claim.

Mechanism / what changed since v2-inline
========================================

  - The single sysctl net.core.flow_dissector_fastpath is replaced by
    a per-shape subtree at /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/{eth_ip, vlan,
    qinq, vxlan_inner}. Each is its own DEFINE_STATIC_KEY_FALSE +
    sysctl; operators enable only the shapes they use. (Hard rename;
    the old knob only existed in net-next 7.1-rc4, no released kernel.)
  - Why per-shape: different deployments want different shapes (DC cake
    on VLAN trunks -> vlan/qinq; k8s overlay -> vxlan_inner; ISP ->
    pppoe later). One coarse all-or-nothing knob doesn't fit. Each gate
    is a separate static_branch (one not-taken JMP when off).

Patch-by-patch
==============

  Patch 1: opt-in fast-path for eth + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP (the
  common-case eth_ip shape). flow_dissect_fast_ipv4/_ipv6 are separate
  static helpers; forward-declared at top of file so the extension
  patches add cleanly. DEFINE_STATIC_KEY_FALSE(flow_dissector_eth_ip_key),
  sysctl /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/eth_ip; a late_initcall registers
  the net/flow_dissector subtree (pattern from net/mpls/af_mpls.c).

  Patch 2: single VLAN (ETH_P_8021Q/8021AD outer tag). byte-identical.
  Own sysctl .../vlan.

  Patch 3: QinQ depth-2 (outer -> KEY_VLAN, inner -> KEY_CVLAN).
  byte-identical. Own sysctl .../qinq; auto-toggle proc_handlers tie
  qinq<->vlan (echo 1 > qinq enables vlan; vlan=0 clears qinq).

  Patch 4: VXLAN inner descent, RFC EXPERIMENT. Independently gated.
  NOT byte-identical (it descends into the inner flow) - gated
  separately so the byte-identical knobs don't inherit the behaviour
  change. Open questions on slow-path symmetry and per-netns port
  discovery.

  Base: net-next at c0aa5f13826d.

Open items
